  "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(2)+{f}^{\u2032}(2)(x-2)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(2)}{2}(x-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-2)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{\u2033}(2).\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-2{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+4{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at $x=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(2) & =-2{e}^{-{2}^{2}}+4\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4}+16{e}^{-4} \\\\  & =14{e}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-2)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(2)}{2} & =\\frac{14{e}^{-4}}{2}=7{e}^{-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
